<DocsLayout
  title="Sparks & limits — Golem Docs"
  description="How Golem's Sparks quota works: 60 per day free, per-mode costs, live metering, daily reset, and exactly what happens when you run out."
  heading="Sparks &amp; limits"
>
  <p>
    Sparks are Golem's unit of daily energy. Every AI request burns some; your balance refills to
    full once a day. The system exists for one reason: real inference costs real compute, and a
    hard, transparent quota is what lets the free tier be genuinely free — no card, no trial
    clock, no surprise bill.
  </p>

  <h2>The numbers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Free plan:</strong> 60 Sparks per day.</li>
    <li><strong>Pro plan (waitlist):</strong> 400 Sparks per day, plus a priority queue.</li>
    <li><strong>Clay:</strong> 1 spark per request.</li>
    <li><strong>Stone:</strong> 4 sparks per request.</li>
    <li><strong>Rune:</strong> 10 sparks per request, <strong>+1 per extra agent step</strong> beyond the included ones.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In practice, 60 Sparks is a solid daily session: dozens of Clay questions, a bunch of Stone
    features, or a few serious Rune runs — usually a mix.
  </p>

  <h2>How metering works</h2>
  <p>
    Your balance is always visible in the workspace header, and it updates live. A request is
    charged when it starts; Rune's per-step surcharge accrues visibly during the run. If Golem
    estimates a Rune run will be unusually long, it says so before starting instead of silently
    draining your day.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a request fails on our side — an infrastructure error, not a build that turned out wrong —
    the Sparks come back automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>The daily reset</h2>
  <p>
    Quotas reset every 24 hours per account, on a rolling clock. The workspace shows your exact
    reset time. Unused Sparks do not roll over — every day starts at your full allowance.
  </p>

  <h2>Running out</h2>
  <p>When your balance hits zero:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>New AI requests are declined with a clear message and your reset time — never a silent failure.</li>
    <li>Everything that does not burn inference keeps working: reading chat history, browsing your project, creating and restoring checkpoints, pairing Studio.</li>
    <li>Nothing is deleted, downgraded or lost. You are just out of energy until the reset.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Other limits worth knowing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rate limiting:</strong> requests are queued per account. Under heavy load, free-tier requests may briefly wait; Pro (when it ships) queues ahead.</li>
    <li><strong>Checkpoints:</strong> stored per project with a rolling cap — the oldest automatic checkpoints are pruned first. Manual checkpoints outlive automatic ones.</li>
    <li><strong>One account per person:</strong> multiplying free accounts to farm quota is against the <a href="/terms">terms</a> and gets caught.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Making Sparks go further:</strong> plan with Clay before building with Rune; give
      specific requests (“the door in Workspace.Lobby”) rather than vague ones; and let Stone do
      the ordinary building — Rune shines when verification matters, not on every task.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
